\title{Text-Message Reminders for Blood-Pressure Medication
Adherence: A Pragmatic Trial in General Practice}
\author{S.~Aldana and K.~Whitfield}
\date{June 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We tested whether weekly text-message reminders improve adherence
to blood-pressure medication among adults registered with general
practices. Recruitment through general practices was slower than
anticipated, and the final sample was 214 patients. Adherence,
measured by prescription refill records, was modestly higher in the
reminder arm than in the usual-care arm. Text-message reminders are
a low-cost way to support adherence, though effects in this
pragmatic trial were modest.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
Poor adherence to blood-pressure medication remains a major cause of
preventable strokes and heart attacks. Reminder systems are cheap
and popular, but evidence from routine general practice is thin.
We ran a pragmatic trial nested in everyday practice work, using
refill records rather than self-report to measure adherence.

\section{Methods}
Adults on long-term blood-pressure medication were enrolled through
their general practices and allocated to weekly text-message
reminders or usual care. Practices screened their patient lists and
sent invitations on our behalf. Adherence over six months was
measured from prescription refill records, with practices blind to
the analysis plan.

\section{Results}
Recruitment reached 214 patients across nine practices. Refill-based
adherence over six months was modestly higher in the reminder arm.
The pattern was similar across age bands, and no practice-level
clustering changed the conclusion.

\input{sections/discussion}

\section{Conclusion}
Weekly text-message reminders gave a modest but real improvement in
refill-based adherence in routine general practice. Larger studies
with longer follow-up are needed to judge whether the effect
persists and at what cost per patient.

\end{document}
